course: dictionary
lesson: 11867
title: Words for people who love books
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bookworm is someone who loves reading and reads a lot of books. «Maya is a real bookworm: she has read every book on the fantasy shelf.» Here bookworm describes a person, and it is said kindly.
explain: Bookworm is an informal word, so you hear it in conversation and school, not so often in formal writing. You can say someone is «a bit of a bookworm» if they read quite a lot, or «a real bookworm» if they read a huge amount.
explain: Check the clues when you meet bookworm. Ask who is being described and what they are doing. If the sentence is about reading, bookworm means the person. If the sentence is about holes in pages, it means the insect.
fact: Bookworm is an informal noun, and its plural is bookworms.
fact: «Lena is a real bookworm» means Lena loves reading and reads a lot.
fact: «The old atlas was full of holes: a bookworm had chewed through it» uses the second meaning, a small insect larva that eats pages and bindings.
fact: Useful phrases: «a real bookworm» and «a bit of a bookworm».
fact: Context tells you which meaning is right, so read the words around the word first.
exercise: «Ravi finished three novels this week. He is such a bookworm.» What does bookworm mean here? | A library worker who stamps the books | *A person who loves reading and reads a lot | A bird that sings in libraries | A small insect that eats through books
exercise: «Two bookworms sat in the corner of the library.» Find and copy the word that means more than one bookworm. | sat | corner | *bookworms | library
exercise: «The old atlas was full of holes: a bookworm had chewed through it.» What does bookworm mean in this sentence? | A shelf in a bookshop | A map lover who collects atlases | A clever child who reads in the dark | *A small insect larva that eats through book pages
test: «Grandma says Sam is a bookworm, and she says it with a smile.» Why is this a kind thing to say? | *Because it praises him for loving reading | Because it means he is lazy | Because it means he is very loud | Because it means he is an insect
test: Which phrase means someone who reads quite a lot, but not all the time? | *a bit of a bookworm | a book-shaped cake | a library card | a real bookworm
exam: «The bookworm in the attic had eaten its way through the old diaries.» Which meaning fits best? | A cat that sleeps on old books | A shelf full of diaries | *A small insect larva that eats through paper | Only a person who reads diaries all day
